    "blocks": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "What will you do if you come across an English word or phrase you don’t know? You’d probably look it up in the dictionary, wouldn’t you? This, however, was not the case 500 years ago. Although English has a history of over 2,500 years, the first English dictionary didn’t appear until the 17th century."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "During the 16th century, Europe saw rich developments in science, medicine, and the arts. People had to “invent” words to express new ideas. Many new English words were borrowed from foreign languages. There was also a "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "renewed",
            "ref": "v-renewed"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " interest in ancient languages. Some new English words had roots in languages like "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Latin",
            "zh": "拉丁语"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " and "
          },
          {
            "t": "gloss",
            "v": "Hebrew",
            "zh": "古希伯来语"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "The general public, however, had difficulty understanding these new words. At that time, few girls attended school. Only wealthy parents could afford professional teachers. Robert Cawdrey, an English schoolteacher, wanted to help. In 1604, he published "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "A Table Alphabeticall"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " in London. Cawdrey hoped his dictionary would help more people learn to read and write “properly”."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "But Cawdrey’s dictionary was not very "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "reliable",
            "ref": "v-reliable"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ". The first "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "edition",
            "ref": "v-edition"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " had only 120 pages. There were only 2,543 words in it. As Cawdrey’s focus was on words “hard for the general public”, many common words were not included."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "And for many words, the explanations were inaccurate. Take “oats” as an example. The definition read,“Something that is generally given to horses in England, but in Scotland supports the people.” For some words, Cawdrey used only one word to explain each of them. This made "
          },
          {
            "t": "em",
            "v": "A Table Alphabeticall"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " more like a book of "
          },
          {
            "t": "vocab",
            "v": "synonyms",
            "ref": "v-synonym"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": " than a real dictionary. While readers could check the spelling of some words using the book, they had no way of finding out what these words actually meant."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "Although Cawdrey’s dictionary had many problems, it was quite popular. As the saying goes, “"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": "____________________"
          },
          {
            "t": "text",
            "v": ".” In 1604, people finally had something to help them read more easily!"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
